{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786428947,"nonce":"k1h66tcxTQYk+pUEAAU2J1pUt4w=","message":"Fixed. SPEC §G.7 now states the implemented order — dead, frozen, unsequenced, already-resolved, root-never-landed, awaiting-root (2s), awaiting-predecessor (1s), proceed — with the verdict table, the 15-minute MaxWait measured from delivery.created_at (it exists because a River snooze consumes no attempt, so the attempt ceiling can never end a wait), and the dead-letter terminal outcome. The three-phase send is documented with a per-phase failure table: the claim commits BEFORE the network call, and TX 2 guards MarkSent on WHERE status='sending' so a lost claim records nothing and increments oto_delivery_claim_lost_total. last_sent_seq moves only in TX 2.\n\nLanded with ADR 0023, plus ORCHESTRATION.md and the CONTEXT.md module map.\n\nReported, NOT fixed — the same wrong instruction still sits in the package a future implementer reads first:\n- ordering/gate.go:190-197 Claim's doc comment still specifies the single-transaction send e776716 removed.\n- ordering/ordering.go:166-175 Decide's comment calls itself 'one deliberate deviation from SPEC §G.7.2' — no longer true now that §G.7.2 is amended.\n- SPEC AC-34 (SPEC.md:3654, mirrored into schema.d.ts) names oto_thread_recovered_total; the real metric is oto_thread_gap_recovered_total, and three others are missing from that list.","files":null}]}